  "subject": "🎒 Axios Finish Line: Teaching AI",
  "date": "Thu, 08 Oct 2026 21:29:30 -0400",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n 🛒 Plus: Random kindness | Thursday, October 08, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Axios Finish Line \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n By Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei &middot; Oct 08, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n Welcome back! Tonight's column first appeared in Axios CEO Jim VandeHei's new weekly C-Suite newsletter . \n\n If you're a CEO or on a CEO's team: Apply now to join C-Suite. Smart Brevity™ count: 600 words … 2½ mins. Copy edited by Amy Stern. \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 1 big thing — A new college course proposal: &quot;Living AI&quot; \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images\n\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Jim writes: \n\n I met with faculty and the AI task force at Furman University in Greenville, S.C., and got a smart, provocative question: What do I wish liberal arts colleges taught students about AI to prep them for working?\n\n In other words, what do CEOs want and need based on the known-knowns of AI capabilities today and our evolving expectations? 💭 My dream: Every college student, from Day 1, takes a course every semester called \"Living AI.\" It's constantly updated — how to use the newest models and how to grapple with their ethical, philosophical and practical consequences.\n\n It fits into a three-track approach:\n\n 1. 🧠 Think without it. In most classrooms, greatly limit or ban AI. Courses built to deepen and widen thinking should force students to read, reason and write on their own. We need thinkers and problem-solvers.\n\n In an AI-saturated world, original thinking becomes your differentiator. You can't steer or judge AI's output if you don't know anything about the world yourself. 2. 🤖 Work with it. Every major should graduate students with real expertise in using AI for that specific field.\n\n A marketing grad should run AI like a pro for marketing. Same for biology, finance, history, nursing. This means faculty teaching field-specific application, not just the generic tutorial. 3. 💡 Live with it. That's the Living AI course concept for everyone.\n\n Hire realists. Find clear-thinking, practical teachers who use and track the models and capabilities, not doomers or cheerleaders. People who marvel at its new capabilities, then clinically assess the real-world impact. Keep it alive. This can't be a static course. The models change constantly, and capabilities grow exponentially. So the \"Living\" part is vital: Treat it like an ever-evolving topic. Try to match the pace of the class with the pace of tech, which is hard but doable. Teach beyond the tech. These students will face wild philosophical and ethical questions: What is consciousness? Can a machine be a friend? What counts as your own work? Who is responsible when an AI agent screws up? Use each week's AI news to tee up those debates. Treat it as living history. No one gets to sit this one out. This is our electricity moment. It's a generational challenge and opportunity. The course should push students to think about shaping AI to benefit society, not just themselves. 👀 Worth a read: The New York Times dug into research on students' brains on AI (gift link). \n\n This, buried midway through the story, should be stamped on every classroom wall: \"These studies so far agree: Artificial intelligence is affecting how people, especially the young, learn and remember. Whether we will be dumber as a result might depend on how — not if — we use AI.\" Share this story . \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA MESSAGE FROM AXIOS\r\n\n \r\n See what's next for media in 2027 \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Media Trends Executive Annual members receive the 2026 Annual Report, our special media document featuring global forecasts, sector analysis and exclusive media data insights.\r\n\n\r\n \r\n🔒 Get the report by becoming a Media Trends Executive member.\r\n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 2. 🍦 &quot;You deserve the best&quot; \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Natalie Peeples/Axios\n\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Thanks to readers who've sent stories of random acts of kindness they've done, received or witnessed. Here's one from Gail Cohen of Savoy, Ill., that made us smile:\n\n \"Browsing the usual selection of low-priced gallons of ice cream, I was about to open the freezer case door to retrieve one. 'Get a better ice cream,' a stranger said, to which I replied, 'It's not in my budget.' The woman took out her wallet and handed me $5, saying 'You deserve the best.'\" 📬 Keep the kindness coming to finishline@axios.com — and don't forget to include your name and hometown!\n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n 🏁 Please invite your friends to join Finish Line . \n\n \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
