V0bV9jb250ZW50PWFtJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/598cdd4c8cc2b200398b463bBc9689df0\" target=\"_blank\">C-Suite newsletter</a>. </p><ul><li><strong>If you're a CEO</strong> or on a CEO's team: <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47818573.62423/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuYXhpb3MuY29tL25ld3NsZXR0ZXJzL2Mtc3VpdGU_dXRtX21lZGl1bT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fY2FtcGFpZ249Y3N1aXRlJnV0bV9jb250ZW50PWFtJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/598cdd4c8cc2b200398b463bCc9689df0\" target=\"_blank\">Apply now</a> to join C-Suite.</li></ul><p><em>Smart Brevity™ count: 600 words … 2½ mins. Copy edited by Amy Stern.</em></p></td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0;border-bottom:10px solid #f5f5f5\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr>      <tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 10px;line-height:1.5\">\r\n<span class=\"b k\" style=\"font-size:27px;font-family:'gorditamedium',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px;line-height:1.5\">   1 big thing — A new college course proposal: &quot;Living AI&quot; </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>   <tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td> <img src=\"https://images.axios.com/6JBgvyIaLUwEW_mknVrMuhK68Ag=/0x0:1280x720/2026/09/28/1790622978762.gif\" width=\"600\" style=\"width:100%\" id=\"item02\" alt=\"Animated Illustration of cursors switching and morphing on top of a column style pedestal. \"> </td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td style=\"padding:0 3.5%;line-height:1.3\">\r\n<span class=\"b\" style=\"font-size:13px;color:#848484\"> <p>Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images</p> </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"15\" style=\"height:15px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr> <tr>\r\n<td class=\"v\" style=\"padding:0 3.5%;line-height:1.75;font-size:17px;font-family:Georgia,serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px\"><p><em>Jim writes:</em></p><p><strong>I met with faculty</strong> and the AI task force at Furman University in Greenville, S.C., and got a smart, provocative question: What do I wish liberal arts colleges taught students about AI to prep them for working?</p><ul><li><strong>In other words, </strong>what do CEOs want and need based on the known-knowns of AI capabilities today and our evolving expectations?</li></ul><p>💭 <strong>My dream: </strong>Every college student, from Day 1, takes a course every semester called \"Living AI.\" It's constantly updated — how to use the newest models and how to grapple with their ethical, philosophical and practical consequences.</p><p><strong>It fits into</strong> a three-track approach:</p><p><strong>1. </strong>🧠 <strong>Think without it.</strong> In most classrooms, greatly limit or ban AI. Courses built to deepen and widen thinking should force students to read, reason and write on their own. We need thinkers and problem-solvers.</p><ul><li><strong>In an AI-saturated world, </strong>original thinking becomes your differentiator. You can't steer or judge AI's output if you don't know anything about the world yourself.</li></ul><p><strong>2. 🤖 Work with it.</strong> Every major should graduate students with real expertise in using AI for that specific field.</p><ul><li><strong>A marketing grad </strong>should run AI like a pro for marketing. Same for biology, finance, history, nursing. This means faculty teaching field-specific application, not just the generic tutorial.</li></ul><p><strong>3. 💡 Live with it. </strong>That's the Living AI course concept for everyone.</p><ul><li><strong>Hire realists. </strong>Find clear-thinking, practical teachers who use and track the models and capabilities, not doomers or cheerleaders. People who marvel at its new capabilities, then clinically assess the real-world impact.</li><li><strong>Keep it alive.</strong> This can't be a static course. The models change constantly, and capabilities grow exponentially. So the \"Living\" part is vital: Treat it like an ever-evolving topic. Try to match the pace of the class with the pace of tech, which is hard but doable.</li><li><strong>Teach beyond the tech. </strong>These students will face wild philosophical and ethical questions: What is consciousness? Can a machine be a friend? What counts as your own work? Who is responsible when an AI agent screws up? Use each week's AI news to tee up those debates.</li><li><strong>Treat it as living history. </strong>No one gets to sit this one out. This is our electricity moment. It's a generational challenge and opportunity. The course should push students to think about shaping AI to benefit society, not just themselves.</li></ul><p><strong>👀 Worth a read:</strong> The New York Times <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47770277.\" target=\"_self\">dug into research</a> on students' brains on AI <em>(gift link).</em></p><ul><li><strong>This, buried midway</strong> through the story, should be stamped on every classroom wall: \"These studies so far agree: Artificial intelligence is affecting how people, especially the young, learn and remember. Whether we will be dumber as a result might depend on how — not if — we use AI.\"</li></ul><p><em><a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47856894.412615/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuYXhpb3MuY29tLzIwMjYvMTAvMDUvbGl2aW5nLWFpLWNvbGxlZ2UtY291cnNlLXByb3Bvc2FsP3V0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB59ea54e7\" target=\"_blank\">Share this story</a>.</em></p></td>\r\n</tr> <tr><td align=\"center\" style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 25px\">   <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47856894.412615/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZmFjZWJvb2suY29tL3NoYXJlci5waHA_dT1odHRwczovL3d3dy5heGlvcy5jb20vbmV3c2xldHRlcnMvYXhpb3MtZmluaXNoLWxpbmUtMjI5YzkxNzItYjQ1OC00MzQyLWI0NWEtNmYzMzQxYjZhYmUzLmh0bWw_Y2h1bmslM0QxJTI2dXRtX3Rlcm0lM0RmYnNvY2lhbHNoYXJlJTIzc3RvcnkxJnV0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB21e14741\" style=\"display:inline-block\"><img width=\"32\" height=\"32\" style=\"border:0\" src=\"https://static.axios.com/img/axios-site/newsletters/logos/footer/share-facebook-circle-border.png\" alt=\"Share on Facebook\"></a> <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47856894.412615/aHR0cHM6Ly90d2l0dGVyLmNvbS9pbnRlbnQvdHdlZXQ_\" style=\"display:inline-block;margin-left:7px\"><img width=\"32\" height=\"32\" style=\"border:0\" src=\"https://static.axios.com/img/axios-site/newsletters/logos/footer/share-x-circle-border.png\" alt=\"Tweet this Story\"></a> <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47856894.\" style=\"display:inline-block;margin-left:7px\"><img width=\"32\" height=\"32\" style=\"border:0\" src=\"https://static.axios.com/img/axios-site/newsletters/logos/footer/share-linkedin-circle-border.png\" alt=\"Post to LinkedIn\"></a>  <a href=\"mailto:?subject=From Axios:  1 big thing — A new college course proposal: &quot;Living AI&quot;&body=teruyuki%40hh.iij4u.or.jp%20has%20shared%20an%20Axios%20story%20with%20you%3A%0A%0A 1 big thing — A new college course proposal: &quot;Living AI&quot;%0Ahttps%3A%2F%2Fwww.axios.com%2Fnewsletters%2Faxios-finish-line-229c9172-b458-4342-b45a-6f3341b6abe3.html%3Fchunk%3D1%26utm_term%3Demshare%23story1\" style=\"display:inline-block;margin-left:7px\"><img width=\"32\" height=\"32\" style=\"border:0\" src=\"https://static.axios.com/img/axios-site/newsletters/logos/footer/share-email-circle-border.png\" alt=\"Email this Story\"></a> <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47856894.\" style=\"display:inline-block;margin-left:7px\"><img width=\"32\" height=\"32\" style=\"border:0\" src=\"https://static.axios.com/img/axios-site/newsletters/logos/footer/share-sms-circle-border.png\" alt=\"Text this Story\"></a></td></tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0;border-bottom:10px solid #f5f5f5\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr>      \r\